## Cause and tested change

Each skirt is a separate 675-vertex component. Almost every top-row vertex has pure pelvis weighting: 74 vertices in each body. The coat waist has a native pelvis/spine blend and moves differently when the torso bends. The skirt's circular rest rim is also much wider at some angles than the actual coat surface.

The test casts onto actual coat waist triangles, interpolates their bone weights, and tucks the skirt rim 1.5–4.5 mm inside that surface. It changes 76 Friar and 74 Shawl top-row vertices. The largest local rest displacement is 97.4 mm and 122.9 mm respectively; these are changes to the oversized rim, not to the body. Nearby face normals are updated, with no retopology.

The reduced skirt has no retained intermediate vertices within the nominal upper 8 cm fitting band. The next lower vertices are about 13 cm below the rim. This makes the top a long triangular strip and limits smooth tension transfer. The untouched lower fan folds are a separate deformation problem. Setting a crouch morph merely from the action label would not repair the independent rope or these attachment weights, and this pilot does not do that.

## Exact limits and preservation

- Actual current GLB rest, run and pickup frames were rendered with the same camera, light and palette as the baseline.
- Only selected skirt POSITION, JOINTS_0, WEIGHTS_0 and adjacent NORMAL rows change.
- All other accessor payloads are exact, including all morph positions and morph normals.
- All indices, UVs, vertex colours, materials, external texture bytes, scene nodes and native bind data are exact.
- The Friar rope belt and knot are intentionally unchanged; its remaining gap is visible.
- No additional texture, draw call, bone or animation change is introduced.
- No dense motion, prone/crouch, crossfade or reduced-LOD acceptance was run. Preserved morph payloads are not a claim that the new rim weights fit every morphed pose.

The private source is `source/fit_waist.py` with its GLB helper `source/waist_glb.py`. It is a deterministic diagnostic on the exact current exported body, not a promoted `appearances.py` builder hook. If this seam method is reused, it must also be expressed in the final source-build path and tested with the final panel construction.

